<meta type=article title="Scroll-spy in the ToC sidebar (#20)">
</meta>

This article renders with the table-of-contents sidebar on (`toc: true`), so the
build injects the scroll-spy script and the ToC entries gain the active-state
hook as the reader scrolls. The nested sections below exercise the ancestor-trail
highlight (an active `3.1.2`-style entry lights up along with its parents).

<section | Introduction>

Opening prose. The ToC lists this as a top-level entry linking to the section id.

<section | Methods>

The Methods section has sub-sections, which have sub-sub-sections — a three-level
nest so the "you are here" trail has something to walk.

<sub-section | Data>

A sub-section under Methods.

<sub-section | Analysis>

Another sub-section, this one with its own sub-sub-sections.

<sub-sub-section | Regression>

A sub-sub-section — the deepest level. When this is the current section, its ToC
entry is active and both Analysis and Methods carry the trail hook.

<sub-sub-section | Validation>

A second sub-sub-section under Analysis.

<section | Results>

A top-level section with one sub-section.

<sub-section | Findings>

A sub-section under Results.

<section | Conclusion>

The final top-level section. When the reader scrolls to the bottom, this entry
stays active (the graceful end-of-document case).
